# arvo-placement

Where the bits sit. Nothing about what they mean.

Given a declared signature from `arvo-format`, this derives the placement: the machine carrier, and
where the value lives inside it. The one thing to hold onto is that no operation reads any of it. Two
placements of the same declared signature compute the same function, every time, and the derivation
chooses where the value goes rather than what it is.

The output depends on whether the value has its allocation to itself. When it does, there is one
output, the carrier, and the language gives you a standalone sized type whose size you can ask for.
When the value shares its allocation with others, there are three, the carrier and the access width
and the stride, and there is no per-element size to ask about at all: you reach the element through
its carrier, and what is measurable is the stride of the whole allocation, which is a property of the
column rather than of the element in it. The access width is not derivable from the carrier and the
carrier is not derivable from it, so the three are genuinely three answers.

That size observation is the only way the footprint is visible, and it matters that it is visible at
all, because minimising footprint is one of the things a consumer can ask for and an objective nobody
can measure is not an objective.

One consequence surprises people, so it is better said here than found later. Two placements of one
declared signature are two types as far as the language is concerned, since they have different
layouts, and an array cannot hold both. They are the same number and they are not the same
arrangement, and only sameness of the number licenses substituting one for the other inside a bigger
computation.

`no_std`, no allocation, sizes const.

